## Context

Init never overwrites, so canonical doctrine drifts away from what a host installed. Upgrade closes the gap, and it touches two kinds of host artifact that need different handling. Methodology files belong to the package: the running server's copy is the correct one, so the only question is whether to take it. The server pin is the exception: an old server cannot know newer releases, so the pin is judged against a target version the caller names. The brain config belongs to the user: it holds wiring and customizations no canonical copy knows about, and it drifts in three separate ways — sections the contract now requires but the file lacks, shipped sections whose text is an old template version, and a launcher in a legacy shape.

Three parties are involved and each can do only part of the work. The tool holds its own version's canonical bytes and can write host files. The agent can talk to the user but cannot be trusted to reproduce canonical text it was handed. The user is the only one who can approve a change to anything they own. Under the source tree's rule, only install, sync and a consent-gated upgrade may write host files, so the brain config's repairs have to come through here, gated by consent.

The end-to-end brain repair order, and the reason tool responses stay small, are set out in the brain provisioning spec. This spec covers only what upgrade decides inside that flow.

## Strategy

**Detecting drift and applying it are separate calls.** A detection call reads every category, writes nothing and returns status, counts and names only. Applying a change is a separate call that names one category. The user's decision sits between the two calls, so no single invocation can both find drift and act on it, and a detection pass has nothing to apply. The agent shows each category as its own decision; nothing upgrades the whole surface on one yes.

**For package-owned categories the category is the unit, and the tool does the writing.** On a confirmed category the tool writes every differing or missing file from canonical and returns a manifest. Half a category would leave artifacts referencing each other at mismatched versions. The agent never receives canonical bytes, so it cannot paraphrase or invent them. Server entries are judged against their existing launch mode, because the user chose it and switching it is not drift repair.

**The caller names the pin's target, and the tool moves it.** An old server has never seen newer releases; the boot report knows the latest and passes it, and only an exact published version is accepted. Without a target, a local entry is judged against the running version. On a yes the tool changes only the version, keeping the command form and every other byte, since the agent never hand-edits the user's MCP config. The new pin runs only in a new session.

**The brain config is written only with consent and only by insertion.** A yes to the brain category inserts every missing section and touches nothing that is already there. The user approved additions, not edits to text they own, so existing sections, launcher wiring, frontmatter and customizations are never rewritten, reordered or reformatted. This is the source tree's consent-gated write. Inserting tool-side avoids a template round trip through the agent, where text gets garbled.

**Template text is inserted only into the template's own integration.** The bundled template is written for Obsidian. Before inserting, the tool checks the config's integration. If it is Obsidian, the sections go in byte-identical. If it is anything else, the tool inserts nothing and returns the names of the sections the user must adapt with the agent. Pasting Obsidian instructions into a config for another backend would satisfy the parser and still mislead every agent that reads it later.

**An insertion returns markers, matches line endings, and can be repeated safely.** The manifest lists only the inserted markers: the agent needs to know which sections landed, not their text, so zero template bytes is the right amount. Inserted text uses the file's existing line endings, because a mixed file looks fully rewritten in editors and diffs. A re-run with nothing missing inserts nothing and returns an empty manifest, so an interrupted repair is safe to retry.

**Inside the brain config, each outdated section is its own decision, guarded by its exact bytes.** Stale sections and a legacy launcher sit outside the category yes; each is shown as its own before-and-after change and applied alone. The apply carries a byte-exact fingerprint of the before text the user saw; any change since is refused with an instruction to re-fetch the diff, because a whitespace-tolerant match would overwrite an edit made after review. Only text matching a known outdated template version is offered, so customized sections never are.

## Good examples

The user approves the agents category, which has three files that differ and one that is missing. The tool writes all four and returns their names. The agent reports "four agent files updated" without ever having seen what is in them.

A brain config is missing two sections and has one stale section. The user approves the brain category. One call inserts both sections in the file's existing line endings and returns two markers. The stale section is offered separately as a small diff, the user says no, and it stays as it is. A second run of the brain category returns an empty manifest.

The boot report says a pinned project is three releases behind and names the latest. The user says yes, the upgrade call names that version, and the tool changes the version in the entry's package argument. The wrapped Windows launch, the auto-confirm flag and the brain server's entry are byte-identical. The agent reports the move and asks for a new session.

## Bad examples

A yes to the brain category that rewrites the config from the template "to bring it fully current." Every missing section appears, but the user's launcher wiring and an edited section are replaced without their approval.

An insertion manifest that echoes each inserted block "so the agent can confirm it." Tests pass, and the response grows by the full template size on every repair, which is the bloat the flow exists to remove.

A pin move that writes a floating latest tag "so it never falls behind again," or rebuilds the entry in the direct launch form. Today's launch works and the tests pass, but the next session resolves whatever was published that morning, the files no longer match the version running, and a launch form the user's machine needed is gone.

